  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Near record warmth Wednesday with dry weather \ncontinuing through late afternoon.\n\nA mid-level ridge will continue to build east across the Atlantic \nseaboard through mid-week, supporting surface high pressure to \npersist off the eastern Atlantic. Overall this will support a \ncontinuation of dry weather through late afternoon Wednesday with \nperiods of mid/high clouds drifting across the region.\n\nA belt of 25-30 knot southerly flow in the boundary layer will bring \nsome increase in winds tonight through Wednesday, most notably along \nthe lee slopes of the Chautauqua Ridge overnight through Wednesday \nmorning where downslope winds may gust to 30 mph at times.\n\nAs the upper ridge builds further north Wednesday, 850mb \ntemperatures will continue to rise, peaking at about +14C. The \nanomalous warmth aloft will combine with some filtered sunshine, \nmechanical mixing from the southerly breeze, and downslope \ncompressional warming to boost temperatures into the mid to upper \n70s across lower elevations. See Climate section for more \ninformation regarding record temperatures. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers return Wednesday night, with unsettled \nweather and cooler temperatures lasting through next weekend.  \n\nDeep troughing and associated closed low will move into the Great \nLakes for the later half of the work week. A deep southerly flow \noriginating from the Gulf and southern Atlantic Ocean will support a \nplume of moisture into the eastern Great Lakes region Wednesday \nnight through Thursday morning. This deep moisture combined with \nforcing from the surface to 500mb due to a passing cold front will \nsupport widespread showers across the region, and provide some \nmodest relief to the recent dry spell. Rainfall amounts will not be \noverly impressive however given the fast motion of the system and \nnarrow axis of moisture. Most areas will see 0.25\" or less of rain \nWednesday night.\n\nIn the wake of the cold front, expect gusty southwest winds \nnortheast of Lake Erie with gusts of 25 to 35 mph impacting much of \nthe Niagara Frontier on Thursday. \n\nThe trough will move overhead Friday and persist into the weekend, \npossibly evolving into a slow moving mid level low by late Friday \nthrough Saturday over the eastern Great Lakes. The trough and \nassociated forcing and moisture will support a chance of showers \nFriday into the weekend. Ongoing cold advection will also bring a \nnotable downward trend in temperatures, with highs this weekend back \nto near average after the exceptional warmth this week.",
